    "created_at": "2016-03-03T10:04:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-03-03T10:04:49Z",
    "body": "@zcorpan:\n\n> It seems like a good idea to add an example that discusses this. If you like you can take a stab at making a pull request for this. :-)\n\nI'll look into drafting some text and figuring out how a pull request works.\n\n@tabatkins:\n\n> There's no real preference to using the element vs the character. CSS does, however, provide a property for automatically using the char when you use the element.\n\nI'll give the spec a read in a bit.\n\n@zcorpan:\n\n> It's not clear to me that misuse of these elements for presentational purposes has been much of a problem, so maybe it's better to not call it out and just say when these elements are appropriate.\n\nI imagine it's not a problem in practice because superscripts in HTML/CSS are so ugly without tweaking of the `vertical-align` property. This is something best done through alternative glyphs, IMO.\n\nThe raised \"c\" in \"McDonald's\", the raised \"st\" in \"1st\", and the raised \"0\" in \"$1.00\" involve alternative glyphs. It makes more sense for authors to use the `span` element in such cases. On the other hand, these cases might as well be allowed with `sup`, given the `b`, `i`, `s`, `small`, and `u` precedents. (IMO, none of those five elements should be in the spec, but what's done is done.)\n\n@patrickdark:\n\n> (It's arguable whether \"[i]n general, authors should use these elements only if the absence of those elements would change the meaning of the content\" applies especially in light of the first example, involving honorifics, where sub and sup appear to be being used purely for presentation purposes.)\n\nOn second thought, something like 123¹ for a footnote unambiguously requires the superscript to retain its meaning, which means this paragraph doesn't apply.\n",
